# Target C-II — deep high-`t` force cells share one common inverse-`D` train grid

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** high-`t` complete rectangle and phase collapse; linear one-bar/exact-antipodal fork; force-carrying robust fork; deep two-sided cusp cells; linear-rank coercive dipole block; disjoint deep exact-force braid  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_force_block_common_grid_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current high-`t` frontier contains a global ordered train rectangle and, on the no-fixed-margin-TB1 side, positive linear density of arbitrarily deep quantitatively two-sided exact-force cells. These facts can be synchronized without any further thinning.

Put

\[
c={113\over5{,}000{,}000},\qquad c_0={c\over4},\qquad
\rho_0={c\over48},\qquad \delta_0={c\over64}.
\tag{CG.1}
\]

There is one column family `B_0`, chosen once from the high-`t` complete rectangle, with

\[
\boxed{|B_0|>0.0783D-o(D).}
\tag{CG.2}
\]

Every fixed-threshold robust blocker interface `i` has both adjacent rows crossing every `b in B_0` and

\[
\boxed{
0<\theta(\tau_j,b)-{\pi\over3}
<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1})
\quad(j=i,i+1).
}
\tag{CG.3}
\]

Its adjacent row phases satisfy

\[
\boxed{
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
<{28.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{CG.4}
\]

At `rho_0` the robust union has density

\[
>{c_0\over2}D-o(D)=2.825\times10^{-6}D-o(D),
\tag{CG.5}
\]

so either its fixed-margin TB1 side or its wide exact-force side has density at least

\[
{c_0\over4}D-o(D)=1.4125\times10^{-6}D-o(D),
\tag{CG.6}
\]

all against the same `B_0`.

More strongly, if the positive-density fixed-margin TB1 branch does not occur, then for every fixed exact-phase radius `r` there are more than

\[
\boxed{
{c\over32}D-o(D)={113\over160{,}000{,}000}D-o(D)
}
\tag{CG.7}

deep two-sided force centers. For every such center `i`, every row in its whole exact-phase neighborhood

\[
u_{i-r}=u_{i-r+1}=\cdots=u_{i+r+1}
\tag{CG.8}
\]

crosses every column of the same `B_0` with the bound (CG.3). The constant `31.4e6` is independent of `r`.

The center reaction also obeys the absolute bound

\[
\boxed{|G_i|<4{,}436{,}000.}
\tag{CG.9}
\]

Along the growing-depth diagonal `r_D->infinity`, all positive-linearly-many deep-cell row axes lie in one projective interval of width

\[
<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
\tag{CG.10}
\]

while `B_0` itself has projective width below

\[
{3.004\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{CG.11}
\]

Thus the deep cells are globally grid-locked, not merely locally co-phased.

---

## 1. One global `B_0`

Use the oriented phase coordinates from the rectangle phase-collapse theorem. There are rows `A_*` and columns `B_+` with

\[
|B_+|>0.1567D-o(D),
\tag{CG.12}
\]

and

\[
\delta_{ab}:=\beta_b-\alpha_a-{\pi\over3}>0.
\tag{CG.13}
\]

The complete-product structure gives

\[
\boxed{\delta_{ab}=g+r_a+q_b,\qquad g,r_a,q_b\ge0.}
\tag{CG.14}
\]

The global average defect satisfies

\[
\lambda_D
={1\over|A_*||B_+|}\sum_{a,b}\delta_{ab}
<{1.502\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{CG.15}
\]

Set

\[
B_0:=\{b\in B_+:q_b\le2\lambda_D\}.
\tag{CG.16}
\]

At least half of `B_+` survives, proving (CG.2), and every `b in B_0` has

\[
q_b<{3.004\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{CG.17}
\]

This selection depends only on the complete rectangle, not on any later cusp center.

---

## 2. Every fixed-good row is close to `B_0`

For a row `tau_j`, define

\[
S_j:=\sum_{b\in B_+}\delta_{jb}.
\tag{CG.18}
\]

By (CG.14),

\[
S_j=|B_+|(g+r_j)+\sum_bq_b,
\]

so

\[
g+r_j\le {S_j\over|B_+|}.
\tag{CG.19}
\]

A fixed-threshold good interface satisfies

\[
J_i=N_i+L_i+DB_i+S_i+S_{i+1}<H,
\qquad H:=4{,}436{,}000.
\tag{CG.20}
\]

Hence `S_i,S_{i+1}<H`, and (CG.12) gives

\[
{H\over|B_+|}
<{28.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{CG.21}
\]

For `j=i,i+1` and `b in B_0`,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta_{jb}
&=g+r_j+q_b\\
&\le {S_j\over|B_+|}+q_b\\
&<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
\end{aligned}
\]

which proves (CG.3).

Also

\[
\delta_{ib}-\delta_{i+1,b}=\alpha_{i+1}-\alpha_i.
\]

Summing over `B_+` gives

\[
|B_+|\,|\alpha_{i+1}-\alpha_i|
\le S_i+S_{i+1}<H,
\]

which proves (CG.4).

The force-carrying robust families are subfamilies of these fixed-good interfaces, so (CG.3)--(CG.4) hold simultaneously for the whole robust fork. Equation (CG.5) is the robust theorem at `rho_0=c_0/12`, and (CG.6) is its two-way pigeonhole consequence.

---

## 3. Deep exact phase propagates the center bound with no loss

Assume the fixed-margin TB1 branch does not occur. The deep two-sided theorem gives, for every fixed `r`, a family `Q_D^{two}(r)` satisfying (CG.7). Every center is a retained robust exact-force center, so its adjacent rows satisfy (CG.3).

Every interface in `[i-r,i+r]` is exact-antipodal, hence all rows in (CG.8) have exactly the same oriented phase. They all belong to `A_*`, so all cross every `b in B_0`. Therefore the center bound (CG.3) holds unchanged for every row in the neighborhood:

\[
\boxed{
0<\theta(\tau_j,b)-{\pi\over3}
<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1})
}
\tag{CG.22}
\]

for

\[
i-r\le j\le i+r+1,\qquad b\in B_0.
\tag{CG.23}
\]

There is no factor of `r`. The rows are internally noncrossing, the `B_0` columns are internally noncrossing, every cross-pair meets once, and the inherited orientations have one determinant sign. The signed monotone-grid lemma therefore gives one common crossing order throughout each `(2r+2)`-by-`|B_0|` rectangle.

---

## 4. Global inverse-`D` phase collapse of all deep centers

Fix one `b_* in B_0`. For a retained center `i`, let `alpha_i` be its exact neighborhood row phase. By (CG.22),

\[
0<\beta_{b_*}-\alpha_i-{\pi\over3}<K_D,
\]

where

\[
K_D:={H\over|B_+|}+2\lambda_D
<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{CG.24}
\]

Thus for any two retained centers `i,k`,

\[
\boxed{|\alpha_i-\alpha_k|<K_D.}
\tag{CG.25}
\]

This proves (CG.10). Since `beta_b=beta^-+q_b` on `B_0`, (CG.17) gives (CG.11).

So both train directions in the deep-cell scaffold are globally confined to inverse-`D` phase windows.

---

## 5. The deep force geometry survives unchanged

The common-grid argument only reads the existing rectangle coordinates; it deletes no deep center. Every center therefore retains the full two-sided cell package:

- bilateral birth on the two ordered host arcs;
- a `rho_0`-wide central exact-force diamond;
- both selected force bars at projective distance at least `delta_0` from the reaction axis;
- opposite-sign elementary-dipole derivatives with
  \[
  |\ell_{h_i^\pm}(s d^{(i)})|
  \ge2D|s|\sin\delta_0;
  \tag{CG.26}
  \]
- the conditioned two-ray force representation.

At the center, the fixed-good estimate also yields

\[
|G_i|\le DB_i<H,
\]

which is (CG.9).

---

## 6. The linear-rank coercive dipole space is grid-locked

The coercive dipole theorem uses a set `E_D^{coer}(r)` of the same deep two-sided centers with

\[
|E_D^{coer}(r)|>{c\over32}D-o(D).
\tag{CG.27}
\]

For

\[
V_D(r):=\operatorname{span}\{d^{(i)}:i\in E_D^{coer}(r)\},
\]

the normalized actual-diameter derivative map satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\left\|{1\over D}L_D(c_s)\right\|_2
\ge2\sqrt2\sin\delta_0\,\|s\|_2.
}
\tag{CG.28}
\]

Sections 3--4 apply to every basis center. Hence the `Theta(D)`-rank uniformly coercive dipole space can be based entirely at cells whose growing exact-phase neighborhoods all use the same `B_0` scaffold and whose row axes lie in one `O(D^{-1})` interval.

The common grid therefore does not create an approximately invisible collective mode inside the canonical dipole span.

---

## 7. The disjoint deep-force braid is grid-locked too

The disjoint-braid theorem thins the deep centers to `M_D(r)` with pairwise disjoint exact-phase windows and pairwise disjoint four-vertex force diamonds. For fixed `r`,

\[
|M_D(r)|>
\left({c\over512\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4}.
\tag{CG.29}
\]

On a diagonal with `r_D->infinity`, `r_D=o(D^{3/4})`, the same `Omega(D^{1/4})` bound survives. Every selected force-diamond chord from one cell crosses every selected force-diamond chord from every other cell, and the bilateral birth endpoints preserve synchronized order on the two host arcs.

Because this is only a thinning of the deep two-sided family, every disjoint window is still crossed by the same `B_0`, with (CG.22) holding throughout. Hence the diagonal contains

```text
Omega(D^(1/4)) pairwise window-disjoint deep cells
+ pairwise vertex-disjoint central force diamonds
+ a complete crossing braid of all selected diameter bars
+ synchronized bilateral birth order
+ one common >0.0783 D ordered transverse train family B_0
+ pi/3 + O(1/D) angle throughout every selected deep window.
```

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The surviving high-`t` branch has now lost another synchronization escape. It must realize either:

```text
(A) a positive-density fixed-margin TB1 population,
    with a linear robust subpopulation against one common
    inverse-D near-triangular column scaffold;

or

(B) positive linear density of arbitrarily deep exact-phase cells,
    all row axes in one O(1/D) phase window,
    all using the same >0.0783 D ordered column family,
    each center carrying a bilateral central two-sided force diamond,
    a Theta(D)-rank dipole subspace with uniform normalized coercivity,
    and an Omega(D^(1/4)) window/vertex-disjoint complete crossing braid
    whose every deep window uses that same column family.
```

The remaining closure problem is geometric, not a missing compactness, conditioning, disjointness, or phase-synchronization step: force the bilateral diameter endpoint/braid order, or the fixed-margin TB1 bars, to contradict the same ordered inverse-`D` train grid, or turn that incompatibility into an exact unit triangle or finite diameter descent.

No such final incompatibility is proved here. C-II and Level I remain open.

**Disposition:** proved a common-grid version of the robust high-`t` fork and embedded the positive-density deep two-sided force cells, their linear-rank coercive dipole space, and their disjoint complete-crossing braid into one globally phase-collapsed ordered linear-column near-triangular train scaffold. C-II and Level I remain open.